{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-fc28bbdf-6113-4463-82b2-152e4ee7c832","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3dd49e8534ec6d6","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9008-fqg9annm/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9008-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9008 (Make RHOAI more usable for enterprise teams)\n\nStrategy status is \"Refinement Done\", but HLR-1 through HLR-3 are direction-level\nrequirements each gated by an open question, and HLR-1 contains directly\ncontradictory account feedback. The decomposition therefore leads with three\nInvestigation epics (one per open question), gates all implementation work on\ntheir outcomes, and uses conditional branch epics for the bounded HLR-1\nconflict. HLR-4 (whitepaper) is a `docs-authoring` epic that runs last.\n\n## Epic List (main plan)\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n| E001 | Resolve conflicting administration-usability direction | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n| E002 | Define data-scientist onboarding scope and time baseline | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n| E003 | Identify target enterprise tools for integration | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n| E005 | Dashboard onboarding experience for new data scientists | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n| E006 | Notebooks workbench onboarding experience | Implementation | Workbenches | Notebooks | P1 |\n| E007 | Enterprise tooling integration in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n| E008 | Enterprise usability best-practices whitepaper | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | Documentation | P2 |\n\nMain-plan epic count: 7. In addition, 2 conditional branch epics implement the\nHLR-1 outcome once E001 resolves (see \"Conditional Branches\" below); they are\nnot standalone Jira issues and are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigation: admin usability direction\"]\n    E002[\"E002 Investigation: onboarding scope\"]\n    E003[\"E002 Investigation: enterprise tool targets\"]\n    E005[\"E005 Impl: Dashboard onboarding\"]\n    E006[\"E006 Impl: Notebooks onboarding\"]\n    E007[\"E007 Impl: enterprise tooling integration\"]\n    E008[\"E008 Docs: usability whitepaper\"]\n    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E004 Impl: simplify admin config (conditional)\"]\n    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E004 Impl: per-project controls (conditional)\"]\n\n    E001 --> BA\n    E001 --> BB\n    E002 --> E005\n    E002 --> E006\n    E003 --> E007\n    E001 --> E008\n    E005 --> E008\n    E006 --> E008\n    E007 --> E008\n```\n\nMain-plan critical path length: 3 (e.g. E002 → E006 → E008, or E003 → E007 → E008).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → BRANCH-A-E004, E001 → BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The HLR-1 direction decision determines which implementation exists. Outcomes are bounded (simplify vs. fine-grained controls vs. layered), so conditional branch epics are emitted instead of a main-plan implementation. Branch epics carry `gated_by=E001` and `gate_failure_impact`. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The onboarding definition determines what Dashboard-side work exists and what it does. True gate, action `rewrite`. |\n| E002 → E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Notebooks-side onboarding work only exists if the defined scope includes the workbench experience. True gate, action `remove`. |\n| E003 → E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The identified tool list determines the integration scope and approach. True gate, action `rewrite`. |\n| E001 → E008 | Rule 11 + branch rule 2 | The whitepaper must reflect the resolved administration direction. HLR-1's implementation lives in branch epics, which main-plan epics must not depend on, so the whitepaper depends on the Investigation outcome itself. |\n| E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | `docs-authoring` epic blocked by all main-plan Implementation epics — docs describe what was built. Per the docs-authoring priority exception, these edges do not push E008's P2 upstream. |\n| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14/15 analog for investigations | The three unknowns are independent questions with distinct deliverables; they are not resolved by the same experiment, so they are separate Investigations that start in parallel. |\n| (no edge) E005 ∥ E006 | Rule 1/14 | Different components (Dashboard vs. Notebooks), different repos — parallel once unblocked. |\n| (no edge) E005/E006 ∥ E007 | Rule 14 | Independent work streams in different repos/areas after their respective investigations resolve. |\n\nNo onboarding-chain edges: both Dashboard and Notebooks are Konflux-onboarded,\nGA components per architecture context (`rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`,\n`rhoai-3.0/notebooks.md`), so no `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation`, or\n`konflux-onboarding` epics are generated and Rule 25 build-pipeline ACs do not\napply.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nE001's outcome is bounded (≤3 possibilities) and selects the HLR-1\nimplementation:\n\n- **Branch A** — `RHAISTRAT-9008-BRANCH-A-E004.md`: simplify/consolidate exposed\n  administration configuration in the Dashboard (the \"too many knobs\" reading of\n  HLR-1). Carries a Rule 23 rollback/feature-flag AC because it replaces an\n  existing administration surface.\n- **Branch B** — `RHAISTRAT-9008-BRANCH-B-E004.md`: fine-grained per-project\n  administration controls (the \"more controls\" reading of HLR-1).\n- If E001 resolves to a **layered** outcome (simplified defaults plus advanced\n  per-project controls), both branch epics are planned together; the branch\n  files already describe the two halves independently.\n\nBranch epics set `branch`, `gated_by=E001` (also in `dependencies`), and\n`gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its\n`dependencies`; work that depends on the HLR-1 outcome (the whitepaper) depends\non E001 instead.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority (strategy) | Epics | Coverage |\n|-----|---------------------|-------|----------|\n| HLR-1 — administration should be simpler (contradictory feedback) | — (assumed P1) | E001 (Investigation), BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004 | Direction decision + both conditional implementations |\n| HLR-2 — onboarding a new data scientist < 1 day | — (assumed P1) | E002 (Investigation), E005, E006 | Scope definition + Dashboard-side and Notebooks-side implementations |\n| HLR-3 — integrate better with enterprise tooling | — (assumed P1) | E003 (Investigation), E007 | Tool identification + integration implementation |\n| HLR-4 — publish enterprise usability whitepaper | P2 | E008 | Docs-authoring epic, last in DAG |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Component and Team Mapping\n\n- Strategy lists Affected Components as a bare list (Dashboard, Notebooks) with\n  no change descriptions or owners. Owners were derived from architecture\n  context: Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard team, Notebooks → Workbenches team.\n- All components are in the platform (architecture context files exist), so no\n  provisioning/onboarding epics are needed.\n- E002 and E003 are Investigations assigned to component Dashboard / RHOAI\n  Dashboard team as the primary affected surface. E003's downstream\n  implementation (E007) is also assigned to Dashboard because the strategy\n  names only Dashboard and Notebooks as affected; if the tooling investigation\n  lands the integration in a different component, E007 is rewritten (its gate\n  already records this).\n- HLR-4 maps to canonical component **Documentation**. No architecture context\n  file exists for Documentation and the strategy has no Impacted Teams table,\n  so team \"RHOAI Documentation\" is an assumption flagged below.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Impacted Teams table, no\n   Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (no Given/When/Then with\n   \"measured by\" clauses), no Non-Functional Requirements. Team assignments\n   were derived from architecture context and one assumption (Documentation).\n   Epic acceptance criteria below are derived from HLRs, not strategy ACs.\n2. **Missing HLR priorities**: HLR-1..3 carry no priority (\"—\"); P1 was assumed\n   because leadership wants visible usability progress this release. Only\n   HLR-4 is prioritized — and it is P2. Priority inversion risk: the most\n   concrete, immediately actionable deliverable (the whitepaper) is the lowest\n   priority, while the higher-assumed-priority work is directionless until the\n   investigations resolve.\n3. **Status inconsistency / scope trap**: the strategy is marked \"Refinement\n   Done\" yet contains three open questions that gate all implementation work.\n   Refinement is not actually done for HLR-1..3.\n4. **HLR-1 internal contradiction**: one account wants fewer configuration\n   options, another wants more fine-grained controls. Handled via E001 with\n   bounded conditional branches rather than picking a winner during\n   decomposition.\n5. **Unbounded scope**: \"Out of Scope: Nothing has been explicitly scoped out.\"\n   Combined with leadership pressure for visible progress, this is a scope-trap\n   risk; the investigations implicitly bound it.\n6. **Strategy priority \"Normal\"** vs. \"leadership wants visible usability\n   progress this release\" — mild tension worth confirming with the strategy\n   owner.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing in this\nstrategy: no upstream PR/RFC epic exists, and HLR-3's tooling unknown is a\nscoping question (E003), not a timed external dependency. If E003 later\nidentifies a tool requiring upstream work, a tiered split can be evaluated at\nthat point.\n\n## Signal Scoring Notes\n\n- All three Investigations are heavily human-judgment-bound (stakeholder /\n  account / product decisions), so their `investigation_signals` score low on\n  `human_judgment_required` — they route to a person, not the AI investigation\n  skill.\n- Implementation epics score low on `change_specificity` / `open_questions`\n  because their scope is genuinely undefined until the gating Investigations\n  resolve; this is expected for a strategy at this fidelity level and is the\n  reason the Investigations lead the DAG.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":30820,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8405694652183ce9","agent_id":"a84dc4f3fbe0fa09d","session_id":"18366dc2-8783-4e47-973f-83e0b584ee1e","uuid":"8ccfabcd-e4b8-4fad-8a69-d151814002a4","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:38:00.515Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9008"}
