{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98fbb94e573260d4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9008\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9008 (Make RHOAI more usable for enterprise teams)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status is \"Refinement Done\", but HLR-1 through HLR-3 are direction-level\n13\trequirements each gated by an open question, and HLR-1 contains directly\n14\tcontradictory account feedback. The decomposition therefore leads with three\n15\tInvestigation epics (one per open question), gates all implementation work on\n16\ttheir outcomes, and uses conditional branch epics for the bounded HLR-1\n17\tconflict. HLR-4 (whitepaper) is a `docs-authoring` epic that runs last.\n18\t\n19\t## Epic List (main plan)\n20\t\n21\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n22\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n23\t| E001 | Resolve conflicting administration-usability direction | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n24\t| E002 | Define data-scientist onboarding scope and time baseline | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n25\t| E003 | Identify target enterprise tools for integration | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n26\t| E005 | Dashboard onboarding experience for new data scientists | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n27\t| E006 | Notebooks workbench onboarding experience | Implementation | Workbenches | Notebooks | P1 |\n28\t| E007 | Enterprise tooling integration in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n29\t| E008 | Enterprise usability best-practices whitepaper | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | Documentation | P2 |\n30\t\n31\tMain-plan epic count: 7. In addition, 2 conditional branch epics implement the\n32\tHLR-1 outcome once E001 resolves (see \"Conditional Branches\" below); they are\n33\tnot standalone Jira issues and are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: admin usability direction\"]\n40\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: onboarding scope\"]\n41\t    E003[\"E003 Investigation: enterprise tool targets\"]\n42\t    E005[\"E005 Impl: Dashboard onboarding\"]\n43\t    E006[\"E006 Impl: Notebooks onboarding\"]\n44\t    E007[\"E007 Impl: enterprise tooling integration\"]\n45\t    E008[\"E008 Docs: usability whitepaper\"]\n46\t    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E004 Impl: simplify admin config (conditional)\"]\n47\t    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E004 Impl: per-project controls (conditional)\"]\n48\t\n49\t    E001 --> BA\n50\t    E001 --> BB\n51\t    E002 --> E005\n52\t    E002 --> E006\n53\t    E003 --> E007\n54\t    E001 --> E008\n55\t    E005 --> E008\n56\t    E006 --> E008\n57\t    E007 --> E008\n58\t```\n59\t\n60\tMain-plan critical path length: 3 (e.g. E002 → E006 → E008, or E003 → E007 → E008).\n61\t\n62\t## DAG Justification\n63\t\n64\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n65\t|------|------|-----------|\n66\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E004, E001 → BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The HLR-1 direction decision determines which implementation exists. Outcomes are bounded (simplify vs. fine-grained controls vs. layered), so conditional branch epics are emitted instead of a main-plan implementation. Branch epics carry `gated_by=E001` and `gate_failure_impact`. |\n67\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The onboarding definition determines what Dashboard-side work exists and what it does. True gate, action `rewrite`. |\n68\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Notebooks-side onboarding work only exists if the defined scope includes the workbench experience. True gate, action `remove`. |\n69\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The identified tool list determines the integration scope and approach. True gate, action `rewrite`. |\n70\t| E001 → E008 | Rule 11 + branch rule 2 | The whitepaper must reflect the resolved administration direction. HLR-1's implementation lives in branch epics, which main-plan epics must not depend on, so the whitepaper depends on the Investigation outcome itself. |\n71\t| E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | `docs-authoring` epic blocked by all main-plan Implementation epics — docs describe what was built. Per the docs-authoring priority exception, these edges do not push E008's P2 upstream. |\n72\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14/15 analog for investigations | The three unknowns are independent questions with distinct deliverables; they are not resolved by the same experiment, so they are separate Investigations that start in parallel. |\n73\t| (no edge) E005 ∥ E006 | Rule 1/14 | Different components (Dashboard vs. Notebooks), different repos — parallel once unblocked. |\n74\t| (no edge) E005/E006 ∥ E007 | Rule 14 | Independent work streams in different repos/areas after their respective investigations resolve. |\n75\t\n76\tNo onboarding-chain edges: both Dashboard and Notebooks are Konflux-onboarded,\n77\tGA components per architecture context (`rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`,\n78\t`rhoai-3.0/notebooks.md`), so no `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation`, or\n79\t`konflux-onboarding` epics are generated and Rule 25 build-pipeline ACs do not\n80\tapply.\n81\t\n82\t## Conditional Branches\n83\t\n84\tE001's outcome is bounded (≤3 possibilities) and selects the HLR-1\n85\timplementation:\n86\t\n87\t- **Branch A** — `RHAISTRAT-9008-BRANCH-A-E004.md`: simplify/consolidate exposed\n88\t  administration configuration in the Dashboard (the \"too many knobs\" reading of\n89\t  HLR-1). Carries a Rule 23 rollback/feature-flag AC because it replaces an\n90\t  existing administration surface.\n91\t- **Branch B** — `RHAISTRAT-9008-BRANCH-B-E004.md`: fine-grained per-project\n92\t  administration controls (the \"more controls\" reading of HLR-1).\n93\t- If E001 resolves to a **layered** outcome (simplified defaults plus advanced\n94\t  per-project controls), both branch epics are planned together; the branch\n95\t  files already describe the two halves independently.\n96\t\n97\tBranch epics set `branch`, `gated_by=E001` (also in `dependencies`), and\n98\t`gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its\n99\t`dependencies`; work that depends on the HLR-1 outcome (the whitepaper) depends\n100\ton E001 instead.\n101\t\n102\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n103\t\n104\t| HLR | Priority (strategy) | Epics | Coverage |\n105\t|-----|---------------------|-------|----------|\n106\t| HLR-1 — administration should be simpler (contradictory feedback) | — (assumed P1) | E001 (Investigation), BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004 | Direction decision + both conditional implementations |\n107\t| HLR-2 — onboarding a new data scientist < 1 day | — (assumed P1) | E002 (Investigation), E005, E006 | Scope definition + Dashboard-side and Notebooks-side implementations |\n108\t| HLR-3 — integrate better with enterprise tooling | — (assumed P1) | E003 (Investigation), E007 | Tool identification + integration implementation |\n109\t| HLR-4 — publish enterprise usability whitepaper | P2 | E008 | Docs-authoring epic, last in DAG |\n110\t\n111\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n112\t\n113\t## Component and Team Mapping\n114\t\n115\t- Strategy lists Affected Components as a bare list (Dashboard, Notebooks) with\n116\t  no change descriptions or owners. Owners were derived from architecture\n117\t  context: Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard team, Notebooks → Workbenches team.\n118\t- All components are in the platform (architecture context files exist), so no\n119\t  provisioning/onboarding epics are needed.\n120\t- E002 and E003 are Investigations assigned to component Dashboard / RHOAI\n121\t  Dashboard team as the primary affected surface. E003's downstream\n122\t  implementation (E007) is also assigned to Dashboard because the strategy\n123\t  names only Dashboard and Notebooks as affected; if the tooling investigation\n124\t  lands the integration in a different component, E007 is rewritten (its gate\n125\t  already records this).\n126\t- HLR-4 maps to canonical component **Documentation**. No architecture context\n127\t  file exists for Documentation and the strategy has no Impacted Teams table,\n128\t  so team \"RHOAI Documentation\" is an assumption flagged below.\n129\t\n130\t## Health Warnings\n131\t\n132\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Impacted Teams table, no\n133\t   Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (no Given/When/Then with\n134\t   \"measured by\" clauses), no Non-Functional Requirements. Team assignments\n135\t   were derived from architecture context and one assumption (Documentation).\n136\t   Epic acceptance criteria below are derived from HLRs, not strategy ACs.\n137\t2. **Missing HLR priorities**: HLR-1..3 carry no priority (\"—\"); P1 was assumed\n138\t   because leadership wants visible usability progress this release. Only\n139\t   HLR-4 is prioritized — and it is P2. Priority inversion risk: the most\n140\t   concrete, immediately actionable deliverable (the whitepaper) is the lowest\n141\t   priority, while the higher-assumed-priority work is directionless until the\n142\t   investigations resolve.\n143\t3. **Status inconsistency / scope trap**: the strategy is marked \"Refinement\n144\t   Done\" yet contains three open questions that gate all implementation work.\n145\t   Refinement is not actually done for HLR-1..3.\n146\t4. **HLR-1 internal contradiction**: one account wants fewer configuration\n147\t   options, another wants more fine-grained controls. Handled via E001 with\n148\t   bounded conditional branches rather than picking a winner during\n149\t   decomposition.\n150\t5. **Unbounded scope**: \"Out of Scope: Nothing has been explicitly scoped out.\"\n151\t   Combined with leadership pressure for visible progress, this is a scope-trap\n152\t   risk; the investigations implicitly bound it.\n153\t6. **Strategy priority \"Normal\"** vs. \"leadership wants visible usability\n154\t   progress this release\" — mild tension worth confirming with the strategy\n155\t   owner.\n156\t\n157\t## Tiered Delivery\n158\t\n159\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing in this\n160\tstrategy: no upstream PR/RFC epic exists, and HLR-3's tooling unknown is a\n161\tscoping question (E003), not a timed external dependency. If E003 later\n162\tidentifies a tool requiring upstream work, a tiered split can be evaluated at\n163\tthat point.\n164\t\n165\t## Signal Scoring Notes\n166\t\n167\t- All three Investigations are heavily human-judgment-bound (stakeholder /\n168\t  account / product decisions), so their `investigation_signals` score low on\n169\t  `human_judgment_required` — they route to a person, not the AI investigation\n170\t  skill.\n171\t- Implementation epics score low on `change_specificity` / `open_questions`\n172\t  because their scope is genuinely undefined until the gating Investigations\n173\t  resolve; this is expected for a strategy at this fidelity level and is the\n174\t  reason the Investigations lead the DAG.\n175\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a3d582ee432abf1","agent_id":"a718e3aea7ece3ad9","session_id":"18366dc2-8783-4e47-973f-83e0b584ee1e","uuid":"d510bafe-d796-4377-b4cb-34b0ab3005ca","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:44:32.127Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-98fbb94e573260d4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
