c.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\nNo circular dependencies. The diagram (`graph TD`, arrows dependency →\ndependent) matches epic frontmatter exactly: E005/E006 depend on E002, E007 on\nE003, E008 on E001+E005+E006+E007, branch epics on E001; the three\nInvestigations have no dependencies. Every edge is justified in the DAG\nJustification table against the construction rules. Branch files set both\n`branch` (A/B, matching filenames) and `gated_by=E001`, and `E001` is in their\ndirect `dependencies`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its\n`dependencies` — E008 correctly depends on the Investigation E001 itself for\nthe HLR-1 outcome. No konflux/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist,\nso no rule 7/8/10 parallelism violations are possible. Parallel-eligible work\nis preserved: the three Investigations run in parallel, and E005 ∥ E006\n(different components, different repos) are not serialized. Critical path\nlength 3 is appropriate for an M-sized strategy with a docs tail (e.g. E002 →\nE006 → E008). No data/artifact dependency discoverable from epic content is\nmissing from the DAG: every consumer of an Investigation's decision document\nor baseline has an edge to that Investigation. One minor observation: E008's\nadmin-usability section must be validated against delivered HLR-1\nimplementation, which lives in branch epics E008 cannot depend on (by the\nbranch rule); the E001 edge plus the \"technically reviewed against the\nimplemented work\" AC is the sanctioned construction and mitigates this, but\nscheduling could in principle let the whitepaper's admin section finalize\nbefore branch implementation lands.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nEach epic has a single component/team tuple, and different tuples produce\nseparate epics (Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard for E001-E003, E005, E007 and both\nbranches; Notebooks/Workbenches for E006; Documentation/RHOAI Documentation\nfor E008). E005 and E006 split the onboarding work along the component\nboundary rather than duplicating it — Dashboard-side project creation/spawn\nvs. Notebooks-side workbench lifecycle — and both reference the same E002\nbaseline without independently rebuilding it. Branch A and Branch B are\nmutually exclusive implementations of a bounded decision, not scope\nduplication. No work-product type mixing: the whitepaper is isolated in a\n`docs-authoring` epic; the \"update in-product administration guidance\" items\nin the branch epics are incidental to the code change, not content-authoring\ndeliverables. No epic appears to exceed a single sprint.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001 selects\nwhich branch implementation exists (or both, under the layered outcome); E002\ndetermines E005's scope and E006's existence; E003 determines E007's scope and\nexistence. All have downstream dependents. All Implementation epics produce\nconcrete artifacts. Gating metadata is complete and consistent: every epic\nwith a non-null `gated_by` has a `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and\n`fallback_approach` populated and meaningful (E006 `remove`, E005/E007/E008\n`rewrite`, branches `remove` with layered-outcome handling); every epic that\nlists an Investigation in `dependencies` sets `gated_by` to it; every\n`gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` (E005→E002,\nE006→E002, E007→E003, E008→E001, branches→E001).\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nSignal rubrics are applied type-correctly: E001-E003 carry\n`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric) and null `ai_signals`; all\nImplementation epics and branches carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric) and\nnull `investigation_signals`; all nine ai-signals files exist with per-signal\nrationales matching the frontmatter values. Values are consistent with\nstrategy content: E001/E002 score `question_specificity: 1` (bounded,\nenumerable outcomes) while E003 scores 0 (no candidate list — correct, the\nstrategy names no tools); all three score `human_judgment_required: -2`\n(stakeholder/account decisions); the gated implementations score\n`open_questions: -1` and `change_specificity: -1`, which correctly reflects\nscope that is genuinely undefined until the gates resolve; E008 scores\n`human_process_gates: -1` for the docs review/publication process; the branch\nepics' `open_questions: 0` is defensible since the gating decision is resolved\nby construction within each branch. E002's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1`\n(baselining needs a live environment while the gating decision does not) is\nborderline but justified in its rationale.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs are\npresent: BRANCH-A-E004 (which replaces an existing administration surface)\ncarries the rollback/feature-flag AC; E008 carries the technical-review\nsign-off AC; no konflux chain exists, so Rule 25 does not apply (correctly\njustified via architecture context — both components are GA/Konflux-onboarded).\nE002 and E003 include closure ACs (\"does not end with an open question\", \"the\nscope decision explicitly states whether Notebooks-side workbench work is\nincluded\") that make the gates checkable. No epic body references sibling\nepics by draft ID — downstream work is described in terms of the gating\ndecision documents, not \"E00x\" labels.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll strategy scope is covered: the three open questions map one-to-one onto\nthe three Investigations, the HLR-1 contradiction is handled with bounded\nbranches covering all three stated outcomes (A, B, and layered — the summary\nexplicitly plans both branches together under layered), and HLR-4's whitepaper\nis E008. Strategy-level context is carried forward rather than dropped: the\nmissing-priorities assumption, the \"Refinement Done vs. open questions\"\ninconsistency, the unbounded out-of-scope statement, and the priority tension\nare all recorded in Health Warnings and