{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8af04d525ddfb21a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9008\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: E008 (whitepaper) has no edge to the HLR-1 branch implementations its\n10\t    admin section must validate; the E001 edge plus the technical-review AC is the\n11\t    rule-sanctioned construction, but the admin section could in principle be finalized\n12\t    before branch implementation lands\n13\t- severity: minor\n14\t  criterion: Completeness\n15\t  description: E008 team assignment (RHOAI Documentation) is an assumption since the\n16\t    strategy has no Impacted Teams table; explicitly flagged in the decomposition\n17\t    summary, but should be confirmed with the strategy owner\n18\terror: null\n19\t---\n20\t## Review Summary\n21\t\n22\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n23\tRecommendation: accept\n24\t\n25\tThe decomposition correctly translates a low-fidelity, contradiction-laden\n26\tstrategy into a coherent epic DAG. The strategy's three unprioritized HLRs are\n27\teach gated by a strategy open question, and the decomposition leads with one\n28\tInvestigation per open question, gates the implementations with proper\n29\t`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` metadata, and handles the bounded HLR-1\n30\tcontradiction with conditional branch epics rather than picking a winner.\n31\tHLR-4 is correctly typed as `docs-authoring` and runs last. No critical or\n32\tmajor issues found; two minor observations are recorded.\n33\t\n34\t## Criterion Details\n35\t\n36\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n37\tAll four HLRs are covered and traceable: HLR-1 → E001 + BRANCH-A-E004 /\n38\tBRANCH-B-E004 (direction decision plus both bounded implementations, with the\n39\tlayered outcome planning both); HLR-2 → E002 + E005 + E006; HLR-3 → E003 +\n40\tE007; HLR-4 → E008. The traceability matrix is present and matches the epic\n41\tfiles' HLR Traceability sections. Priority handling is sound: HLR-1..3 carry\n42\tno strategy priority, and the assumed P1 is explicitly justified (leadership\n43\twants visible progress) and documented in Health Warnings. HLR-4's P2 maps to\n44\tE008's P2. Priority inheritance holds everywhere it applies (E001/E002/E003 P1\n45\t→ P1 implementations; branch epics P1). E008 is `docs-authoring`, so its P2\n46\tpriority below its P1 dependencies is the documented exemption, not a\n47\tviolation. No priority collapse: each epic maps to a single HLR, and no\n48\tdeferrable lower-priority feature is bundled into a higher-priority epic.\n49\t\n50\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n51\tNo circular dependencies. The diagram (`graph TD`, arrows dependency →\n52\tdependent) matches epic frontmatter exactly: E005/E006 depend on E002, E007 on\n53\tE003, E008 on E001+E005+E006+E007, branch epics on E001; the three\n54\tInvestigations have no dependencies. Every edge is justified in the DAG\n55\tJustification table against the construction rules. Branch files set both\n56\t`branch` (A/B, matching filenames) and `gated_by=E001`, and `E001` is in their\n57\tdirect `dependencies`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its\n58\t`dependencies` — E008 correctly depends on the Investigation E001 itself for\n59\tthe HLR-1 outcome. No konflux/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist,\n60\tso no rule 7/8/10 parallelism violations are possible. Parallel-eligible work\n61\tis preserved: the three Investigations run in parallel, and E005 ∥ E006\n62\t(different components, different repos) are not serialized. Critical path\n63\tlength 3 is appropriate for an M-sized strategy with a docs tail (e.g. E002 →\n64\tE006 → E008). No data/artifact dependency discoverable from epic content is\n65\tmissing from the DAG: every consumer of an Investigation's decision document\n66\tor baseline has an edge to that Investigation. One minor observation: E008's\n67\tadmin-usability section must be validated against delivered HLR-1\n68\timplementation, which lives in branch epics E008 cannot depend on (by the\n69\tbranch rule); the E001 edge plus the \"technically reviewed against the\n70\timplemented work\" AC is the sanctioned construction and mitigates this, but\n71\tscheduling could in principle let the whitepaper's admin section finalize\n72\tbefore branch implementation lands.\n73\t\n74\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n75\tEach epic has a single component/team tuple, and different tuples produce\n76\tseparate epics (Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard for E001-E003, E005, E007 and both\n77\tbranches; Notebooks/Workbenches for E006; Documentation/RHOAI Documentation\n78\tfor E008). E005 and E006 split the onboarding work along the component\n79\tboundary rather than duplicating it — Dashboard-side project creation/spawn\n80\tvs. Notebooks-side workbench lifecycle — and both reference the same E002\n81\tbaseline without independently rebuilding it. Branch A and Branch B are\n82\tmutually exclusive implementations of a bounded decision, not scope\n83\tduplication. No work-product type mixing: the whitepaper is isolated in a\n84\t`docs-authoring` epic; the \"update in-product administration guidance\" items\n85\tin the branch epics are incidental to the code change, not content-authoring\n86\tdeliverables. No epic appears to exceed a single sprint.\n87\t\n88\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n89\tAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001 selects\n90\twhich branch implementation exists (or both, under the layered outcome); E002\n91\tdetermines E005's scope and E006's existence; E003 determines E007's scope and\n92\texistence. All have downstream dependents. All Implementation epics produce\n93\tconcrete artifacts. Gating metadata is complete and consistent: every epic\n94\twith a non-null `gated_by` has a `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and\n95\t`fallback_approach` populated and meaningful (E006 `remove`, E005/E007/E008\n96\t`rewrite`, branches `remove` with layered-outcome handling); every epic that\n97\tlists an Investigation in `dependencies` sets `gated_by` to it; every\n98\t`gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` (E005→E002,\n99\tE006→E002, E007→E003, E008→E001, branches→E001).\n100\t\n101\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n102\tSignal rubrics are applied type-correctly: E001-E003 carry\n103\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric) and null `ai_signals`; all\n104\tImplementation epics and branches carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric) and\n105\tnull `investigation_signals`; all nine ai-signals files exist with per-signal\n106\trationales matching the frontmatter values. Values are consistent with\n107\tstrategy content: E001/E002 score `question_specificity: 1` (bounded,\n108\tenumerable outcomes) while E003 scores 0 (no candidate list — correct, the\n109\tstrategy names no tools); all three score `human_judgment_required: -2`\n110\t(stakeholder/account decisions); the gated implementations score\n111\t`open_questions: -1` and `change_specificity: -1`, which correctly reflects\n112\tscope that is genuinely undefined until the gates resolve; E008 scores\n113\t`human_process_gates: -1` for the docs review/publication process; the branch\n114\tepics' `open_questions: 0` is defensible since the gating decision is resolved\n115\tby construction within each branch. E002's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1`\n116\t(baselining needs a live environment while the gating decision does not) is\n117\tborderline but justified in its rationale.\n118\t\n119\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n120\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs are\n121\tpresent: BRANCH-A-E004 (which replaces an existing administration surface)\n122\tcarries the rollback/feature-flag AC; E008 carries the technical-review\n123\tsign-off AC; no konflux chain exists, so Rule 25 does not apply (correctly\n124\tjustified via architecture context — both components are GA/Konflux-onboarded).\n125\tE002 and E003 include closure ACs (\"does not end with an open question\", \"the\n126\tscope decision explicitly states whether Notebooks-side workbench work is\n127\tincluded\") that make the gates checkable. No epic body references sibling\n128\tepics by draft ID — downstream work is described in terms of the gating\n129\tdecision documents, not \"E00x\" labels.\n130\t\n131\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n132\tAll strategy scope is covered: the three open questions map one-to-one onto\n133\tthe three Investigations, the HLR-1 contradiction is handled with bounded\n134\tbranches covering all three stated outcomes (A, B, and layered — the summary\n135\texplicitly plans both branches together under layered), and HLR-4's whitepaper\n136\tis E008. Strategy-level context is carried forward rather than dropped: the\n137\tmissing-priorities assumption, the \"Refinement Done vs. open questions\"\n138\tinconsistency, the unbounded out-of-scope statement, and the priority tension\n139\tare all recorded in Health Warnings and reflected in epic framing (e.g., E005's\n140\t\"keep scope strictly consistent with the defined onboarding scope\"). The\n141\tfeedback-consolidation need from the problem statement is distributed across\n142\tE001 (HLR-1 feedback), E002 (baseline), and E003 (tool extraction), which is\n143\tappropriate. The strategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section, so no\n144\tdocs-support obligation is unmet — and the whitepaper HLR is covered anyway.\n145\tMinor: the \"RHOAI Documentation\" team for E008 is an assumption (no Impacted\n146\tTeams table exists in the strategy), but it is explicitly flagged as such in\n147\tthe decomposition summary rather than silently asserted.\n148\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa6cafe0284e9231","agent_id":"aa1147dff6df45cba","session_id":"18366dc2-8783-4e47-973f-83e0b584ee1e","uuid":"55a0038e-4483-4dfd-9b9b-94d38b3bf0cb","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:48:47.538Z","tool_result_