{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-867197c0c9f3f60f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9008\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9008 (Make RHOAI more usable for enterprise teams)\n10\t\n11\tStrategy status is \"Refinement Done\", but HLR-1 through HLR-3 are direction-level\n12\trequirements each gated by an open question, and HLR-1 contains directly\n13\tcontradictory account feedback. The decomposition therefore leads with three\n14\tInvestigation epics (one per open question), gates all implementation work on\n15\ttheir outcomes, and uses conditional branch epics for the bounded HLR-1\n16\tconflict. HLR-4 (whitepaper) is a `docs-authoring` epic that runs last.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List (main plan)\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n22\t| E001 | Resolve conflicting administration-usability direction | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n23\t| E002 | Define data-scientist onboarding scope and time baseline | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n24\t| E003 | Identify target enterprise tools for integration | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n25\t| E005 | Dashboard onboarding experience for new data scientists | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n26\t| E006 | Notebooks workbench onboarding experience | Implementation | Workbenches | Notebooks | P1 |\n27\t| E007 | Enterprise tooling integration in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n28\t| E008 | Enterprise usability best-practices whitepaper | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | Documentation | P2 |\n29\t\n30\tMain-plan epic count: 7. In addition, 2 conditional branch epics implement the\n31\tHLR-1 outcome once E001 resolves (see \"Conditional Branches\" below); they are\n32\tnot standalone Jira issues and are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n33\t\n34\t## Dependency DAG\n35\t\n36\t```mermaid\n37\tgraph TD\n38\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: admin usability direction\"]\n39\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: onboarding scope\"]\n40\t    E003[\"E003 Investigation: enterprise tool targets\"]\n41\t    E005[\"E005 Impl: Dashboard onboarding\"]\n42\t    E006[\"E006 Impl: Notebooks onboarding\"]\n43\t    E007[\"E007 Impl: enterprise tooling integration\"]\n44\t    E008[\"E008 Docs: usability whitepaper\"]\n45\t    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E004 Impl: simplify admin config (conditional)\"]\n46\t    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E004 Impl: per-project controls (conditional)\"]\n47\t\n48\t    E001 --> BA\n49\t    E001 --> BB\n50\t    E002 --> E005\n51\t    E002 --> E006\n52\t    E003 --> E007\n53\t    E001 --> E008\n54\t    E005 --> E008\n55\t    E006 --> E008\n56\t    E007 --> E008\n57\t```\n58\t\n59\tMain-plan critical path length: 3 (e.g. E002 → E006 → E008, or E003 → E007 → E008).\n60\t\n61\t## DAG Justification\n62\t\n63\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n64\t|------|------|-----------|\n65\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E004, E001 → BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The HLR-1 direction decision determines which implementation exists. Outcomes are bounded (simplify vs. fine-grained controls vs. layered), so conditional branch epics are emitted instead of a main-plan implementation. Branch epics carry `gated_by=E001` and `gate_failure_impact`. |\n66\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The onboarding definition determines what Dashboard-side work exists and what it does. True gate, action `rewrite`. |\n67\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Notebooks-side onboarding work only exists if the defined scope includes the workbench experience. True gate, action `remove`. |\n68\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The identified tool list determines the integration scope and approach. True gate, action `rewrite`. |\n69\t| E001 → E008 | Rule 11 + branch rule 2 | The whitepaper must reflect the resolved administration direction. HLR-1's implementation lives in branch epics, which main-plan epics must not depend on, so the whitepaper depends on the Investigation outcome itself. |\n70\t| E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | `docs-authoring` epic blocked by all main-plan Implementation epics — docs describe what was built. Per the docs-authoring priority exception, these edges do not push E008's P2 upstream. |\n71\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14/15 analog for investigations | The three unknowns are independent questions with distinct deliverables; they are not resolved by the same experiment, so they are separate Investigations that start in parallel. |\n72\t| (no edge) E005 ∥ E006 | Rule 1/14 | Different components (Dashboard vs. Notebooks), different repos — parallel once unblocked. |\n73\t| (no edge) E005/E006 ∥ E007 | Rule 14 | Independent work streams in different repos/areas after their respective investigations resolve. |\n74\t\n75\tNo onboarding-chain edges: both Dashboard and Notebooks are Konflux-onboarded,\n76\tGA components per architecture context (`rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`,\n77\t`rhoai-3.0/notebooks.md`), so no `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation`, or\n78\t`konflux-onboarding` epics are generated and Rule 25 build-pipeline ACs do not\n79\tapply.\n80\t\n81\t## Conditional Branches\n82\t\n83\tE001's outcome is bounded (≤3 possibilities) and selects the HLR-1\n84\timplementation:\n85\t\n86\t- **Branch A** — `RHAISTRAT-9008-BRANCH-A-E004.md`: simplify/consolidate exposed\n87\t  administration configuration in the Dashboard (the \"too many knobs\" reading of\n88\t  HLR-1). Carries a Rule 23 rollback/feature-flag AC because it replaces an\n89\t  existing administration surface.\n90\t- **Branch B** — `RHAISTRAT-9008-BRANCH-B-E004.md`: fine-grained per-project\n91\t  administration controls (the \"more controls\" reading of HLR-1).\n92\t- If E001 resolves to a **layered** outcome (simplified defaults plus advanced\n93\t  per-project controls), both branch epics are planned together; the branch\n94\t  files already describe the two halves independently.\n95\t\n96\tBranch epics set `branch`, `gated_by=E001` (also in `dependencies`), and\n97\t`gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its\n98\t`dependencies`; work that depends on the HLR-1 outcome (the whitepaper) depends\n99\ton E001 instead.\n100\t\n101\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n102\t\n103\t| HLR | Priority (strategy) | Epics | Coverage |\n104\t|-----|---------------------|-------|----------|\n105\t| HLR-1 — administration should be simpler (contradictory feedback) | — (assumed P1) | E001 (Investigation), BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004 | Direction decision + both conditional implementations |\n106\t| HLR-2 — onboarding a new data scientist < 1 day | — (assumed P1) | E002 (Investigation), E005, E006 | Scope definition + Dashboard-side and Notebooks-side implementations |\n107\t| HLR-3 — integrate better with enterprise tooling | — (assumed P1) | E003 (Investigation), E007 | Tool identification + integration implementation |\n108\t| HLR-4 — publish enterprise usability whitepaper | P2 | E008 | Docs-authoring epic, last in DAG |\n109\t\n110\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n111\t\n112\t## Component and Team Mapping\n113\t\n114\t- Strategy lists Affected Components as a bare list (Dashboard, Notebooks) with\n115\t  no change descriptions or owners. Owners were derived from architecture\n116\t  context: Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard team, Notebooks → Workbenches team.\n117\t- All components are in the platform (architecture context files exist), so no\n118\t  provisioning/onboarding epics are needed.\n119\t- E002 and E003 are Investigations assigned to component Dashboard / RHOAI\n120\t  Dashboard team as the primary affected surface. E003's downstream\n121\t  implementation (E007) is also assigned to Dashboard because the strategy\n122\t  names only Dashboard and Notebooks as affected; if the tooling investigation\n123\t  lands the integration in a different component, E007 is rewritten (its gate\n124\t  already records this).\n125\t- HLR-4 maps to canonical component **Documentation**. No architecture context\n126\t  file exists for Documentation and the strategy has no Impacted Teams table,\n127\t  so team \"RHOAI Documentation\" is an assumption flagged below.\n128\t\n129\t## Health Warnings\n130\t\n131\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Impacted Teams table, no\n132\t   Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (no Given/When/Then with\n133\t   \"measured by\" clauses), no Non-Functional Requirements. Team assignments\n134\t   were derived from architecture context and one assumption (Documentation).\n135\t   Epic acceptance criteria below are derived from HLRs, not strategy ACs.\n136\t2. **Missing HLR priorities**: HLR-1..3 carry no priority (\"—\"); P1 was assumed\n137\t   because leadership wants visible usability progress this release. Only\n138\t   HLR-4 is prioritized — and it is P2. Priority inversion risk: the most\n139\t   concrete, immediately actionable deliverable (the whitepaper) is the lowest\n140\t   priority, while the higher-assumed-priority work is directionless until the\n141\t   investigations resolve.\n142\t3. **Status inconsistency / scope trap**: the strategy is marked \"Refinement\n143\t   Done\" yet contains three open questions that gate all implementation work.\n144\t   Refinement is not actually done for HLR-1..3.\n145\t4. **HLR-1 internal contradiction**: one account wants fewer configuration\n146\t   options, another wants more fine-grained controls. Handled via E001 with\n147\t   bounded conditional branches rather than picking a winner during\n148\t   decomposition.\n149\t5. **Unbounded scope**: \"Out of Scope: Nothing has been explicitly scoped out.\"\n150\t   Combined with leadership pressure for visible progress, this is a scope-trap\n151\t   risk; the investigations implicitly bound it.\n152\t6. **Strategy priority \"Normal\"** vs. \"leadership wants visible usability\n153\t   progress this release\" — mild tension worth confirming with the strategy\n154\t   owner.\n155\t\n156\t## Tiered Delivery\n157\t\n158\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing in this\n159\tstrategy: no upstream PR/RFC epic exists, and HLR-3's tooling unknown is a\n160\tscoping question (E003), not a timed external dependency. If E003 later\n161\tidentifies a tool requiring upstream work, a tiered split can be evaluated at\n162\tthat point.\n163\t\n164\t## Signal Scoring Notes\n165\t\n166\t- All three Investigations are heavily human-judgment-bound (stakeholder /\n167\t  account / product decisions), so their `investigation_signals` score low on\n168\t  `human_judgment_required` — they route to a person, not the AI investigation\n169\t  skill.\n170\t- Implementation epics score low on `change_specificity` / `open_questions`\n171\t  because their scope is genuinely undefined until the gating Investigations\n172\t  resolve; this is expected for a strategy at this fidelity level and is the\n173\t  reason the Investigations lead the DAG.\n174\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa6cafe0284e9231","agent_id":"aa1147dff6df45cba","session_id":"18366dc2-8783-4e47-973f-83e0b584ee1e","uuid":"ef1402ad-e525-4d7a-8434-b02d7dfe90b4","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:49:04.829Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-867197c0c9f3f60f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
